NAME
    search - classic 'first scan, then narrow' memory search

SYNOPSIS
    search <space> <value> [<width>]
    search refine <value>
    search changed|unchanged|increased|decreased
    search list [<count>]
    search reset

DESCRIPTION
    Finds where a game stores something without already knowing the address
    (a score, lives, a flag). Start with 'search <space> <value>' to find
    every address currently equal to <value> (width 1/2/4 bytes, default 1,
    little-endian); then narrow with 'refine' (equal to a new value) or
    changed/unchanged/increased/decreased (relative to the last scan/refine)
    until only the real address remains. One search session at a time -
    starting a new 'search <space> ...' replaces whatever was active.
    Refuses to scan a live-hardware-routed space (e.g. CpuBus) outright,
    since bulk-reading it can have real side effects (RDNMI clearing the
    pending-NMI flag, etc.) - use a plain space like WRAM instead.

EXAMPLES
    search WRAM 64
    search refine 65
    search list
